content:
Image processing combines untrusted bytes, complex decoders and potentially expensive work. A useful design separates upload admission, isolated conversion and public delivery. Each boundary should have a narrow contract and an observable failure state.

## Model the asset lifecycle before choosing services

Use states such as `received`, `validated`, `processing`, `ready`, `failed` and `expired`. The public asset endpoint serves only `ready` outputs. A worker crash must leave an incomplete private result, not a partially written public file. Store an immutable source identifier and a processing-version identifier with each job so that a later retry can identify exactly what it is reproducing.

The upload endpoint checks authentication, authorization, request size and quota. It stores the source under an application-generated key in private quarantine. It then records a durable job. A job payload should identify the object and requested preset, rather than carry a user-supplied shell command or arbitrary output path.

## Keep decoding behind a resource boundary

The following Node.js 24 wrapper demonstrates a fixed converter entry point and safe argument handling. The converter is an application-owned program that must independently validate format and dimensions. The example limits wall time and captured diagnostics; it does not impose CPU or address-space limits. Apply OS or managed-worker resource limits around the converter in production.

```javascript
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';

export function convert({ jobDirectory, workerProgram }) {
  const directory = resolve(jobDirectory);
  return new Promise((resolveJob, rejectJob) => {
    const child = spawn(process.execPath, [workerProgram, 'source.bin', 'result.png'], {
      cwd: directory,
      shell: false,
      windowsHide: true,
      stdio: ['ignore', 'ignore', 'pipe'],
      env: { NODE_ENV: 'production' },
    });
    let diagnosticBytes = 0;
    let timedOut = false;
    const timer = setTimeout(() => {
      timedOut = true;
      child.kill();
    }, 30_000);
    child.stderr.on('data', (chunk) => {
      diagnosticBytes += chunk.length;
      if (diagnosticBytes > 64 * 1024) child.kill();
    });
    child.once('error', (error) => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      rejectJob(error);
    });
    child.once('close', (code) => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      if (code !== 0 || timedOut || diagnosticBytes > 64 * 1024) {
        rejectJob(new Error('Conversion failed within worker limits.'));
      } else resolveJob({ candidate: resolve(directory, 'result.png') });
    });
  });
}
```

Only call this wrapper with a trusted absolute worker-program path and an application-created job directory. Do not populate these parameters directly from an HTTP request. A killed child is not proof that a whole process tree was terminated; use a job object, container or equivalent supervisor when the converter launches descendants. Never expose raw decoder diagnostics to end users or log source pixels and credentials.

## Promote a verified output atomically

After conversion, reopen the candidate through an independent output check. Verify its declared format, dimensions, nonzero length and permitted metadata. Write the final object under an immutable key. Change the database state to `ready` only after the object exists and its checksum is recorded. Readers should not discover output by guessing temporary filenames.

If object storage and the database cannot share a transaction, record the staged object and reconcile it. A crash between upload and database completion can leave an orphan, so a retention process must remove unreferenced private objects after a documented grace period. Reconciliation should reuse an already verified object instead of creating an extra output for the same source and preset.

## Exercise the boundaries

Test decoder failures, timeout, worker termination, storage write failure and a crash immediately before the `ready` transition. Confirm that every failure keeps the asset private. Measure queue age and conversion failure categories, and alert on sustained failures instead of retrying permanently invalid inputs forever.

Use [Validating Image Uploads in JavaScript](../validating-image-uploads-in-javascript/) for the client contract. For an image prepared manually with [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/), treat the downloaded output as a new input to your pipeline and validate it before delivery. No automated product API is assumed. The [alpha-channel guide](../transparent-png-and-alpha-channel-best-practices/) explains output checks that complement the security boundary.

## Primary references

- [Node.js 24: child processes](https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v24.x/api/child_process.html)
- [OWASP: File Upload Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/File_Upload_Cheat_Sheet.html)
